\>", "TableText",ExpressionUUID->"ca7636d3-39c7-471e-a53f-11640e15500f"]}
  }]], "2ColumnTableMod",ExpressionUUID->"943d4c94-3699-46c3-8e27-\
5cd8a5ea43c1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData["All"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "d17c764a-f1df-458a-a394-d75ad602b923"],
 " returns these nine readings. Counts and densities are computed on the \
directed acyclic graph without enumerating its paths. Use the sampler to \
obtain vertex-list members; the graph's states encode history rather than \
substrate vertices alone."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"30c538f9-010e-4ee3-9cee-7a3ab02ba4d4"],

Cell["\<\
When the maximum excess is below the girth of the tube graph, nonbacktracking \
walks within the length bound are exactly the simple paths. The construction \
then has polynomial size. In particular, excess at most 2 has this guarantee \
on every simple graph, and excess at most 3 on a square grid.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"dd284b26-de27-4516-b478-8b8dec840227"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For larger excess, the construction retains enough recent history to \
exclude repeated vertices exactly. Its size can grow exponentially with the \
excess. Reaching ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<MaxStates\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "22db1455-f02f-4683-a8a4-070cb2ebaac5"],
 " leaves the graph reading, dependent measurements and sampling calls \
unevaluated; no partial count is returned. Membership is checked directly and \
does not require constructing the family graph."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"94061444-6e0e-4a2b-b0c2-fbd545aeefd8"]
}, Open  ]],

